</nav></header><main id="main"><div class="wrap"><section class="case-hero"><a class="back" href="index.html#work">← All selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">Lillup / Learning platform · Product discovery</p><h1>Give a broad product a clearer structure.</h1><p class="case-intro">Research organisation, journey mapping and interface exploration for a learning platform with connected onboarding and AI interaction flows.</p><dl class="case-meta"><div><dt>Product</dt><dd>AI-supported learning platform</dd></div><div><dt>My focus</dt><dd>Research · UX/UI</dd></div><div><dt>Core complexity</dt><dd>Connected roles & journeys</dd></div><div><dt>Deliverables</dt><dd>Maps · Flows · UI explorations</dd></div></dl></section><div class="case-cover"><img src="assets/Lillup3.png" alt="Lillup process board with feature flows and design exploration" fetchpriority="high"></div><div class="case-layout"><aside class="case-toc" aria-label="On this page"><p>On this page</p><a href="#challenge">01 / The question</a><a href="#structure">02 / The structure</a><a href="#decisions">03 / Design decisions</a><a href="#outcome">04 / Deliverables & next steps</a></aside><article class="case-content"><section id="challenge"><h2>Start by organising the questions.</h2><p>Lillup brings multiple journeys into one product. Before deciding how individual screens should look, the work needs a clear view of users, entry points and the actions connecting those screens.</p><p><strong>The design question:</strong> how can the team turn a broad product scope into understandable journeys and focused interface decisions?</p></section><section id="structure"><h2>Make the structure visible.</h2><p>The process boards bring together sorting exercises, journey maps, proto-personas and problem statements. These artefacts organise the discovery work and provide a shared reference for the flows.</p><figure><button class="image-button" data-zoom aria-label="Expand image: Overview of Lillup research organisation and journey mapping"><img src="assets/Lillup1.png" alt="Overview of Lillup research organisation and journey mapping" loading="lazy"></button><figcaption>Selected process overview. The original shared export is low resolution; it shows the structure of the work rather than detailed research findings.<span>Click to expand ↗</span></figcaption></figure><p>Proto-personas are starting assumptions. They need evidence from actual users before they can be treated as validated descriptions of an audience.</p></section><section id="decisions"><h2>Connect the journey to the interface.</h2><div class="decision"><small>Decision 01</small><h3>Map connected actions before styling them.</h3><p>The flow boards show linked steps for onboarding and product interactions. This makes the relationship between entry points, choices and subsequent screens explicit.</p><p class="tradeoff"><strong>The trade-off:</strong> A large flow can become difficult to read. Individual tasks need focused walkthroughs alongside the overall map.</p></div><figure><button class="image-button" data-zoom aria-label="Expand image: Feature and subscription flow planning for Lillup"><img src="assets/Lillup3.png" alt="Feature and subscription flow planning for Lillup" loading="lazy"></button><figcaption>Flow exploration across connected product capabilities.<span>Click to expand ↗</span></figcaption></figure><div class="decision"><small>Decision 02</small><h3>Explore the journey through concrete screens.</h3><p>The mobile explorations connect onboarding and interaction patterns with colour and component choices. Moving into screens makes the proposed journey easier to discuss.</p><p class="tradeoff"><strong>The trade-off:</strong> A coherent visual direction does not establish usability. The next step is observing people attempt the tasks.</p></div><figure><button class="image-button" data-zoom aria-label="Expand image: Lillup mobile onboarding and interaction design exploration"><img src="assets/Lillup6.png" alt="Lillup mobile onboarding and interaction design exploration" loading="lazy"></button><figcaption>Selected mobile interface exploration from the available project export.<span>Click to expand ↗</span></figcaption></figure></section><section id="outcome"><h2>A foundation for product conversations.</h2><ul class="deliverables"><li>Organised discovery artefacts and journey maps.</li><li>Connected user-flow explorations.</li><li>Mobile interface explorations for onboarding and interaction.</li></ul><div class="learning"><p>The value of a process artefact is the decision it helps the team make.</p></div><h3>What I’d test next</h3><p>I would choose a specific onboarding journey, recruit people who match the intended users and observe where they hesitate, backtrack or need help. The next version should connect each meaningful design change to the observation behind it.</p></section></article></div><a class="next-project" href="veritas.html"><div><p class="eyebrow">Next project</p><h3>Veritas / GP Flow</h3></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><section class="contact" id="contact"><div class="wrap contact-layout"><div><p class="eyebrow">A good place to start</p><h2>What are you<br>trying to solve?</h2><p>I’m open to product design roles and focused projects with teams building useful, thoughtful software.</p></div><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="mailto:tatheerulhassan799@gmail.com">Start a conversation <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section></main>
